WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.880
North American mid-rise elevators cost $170,000 to install versus $60,000 or less in Europe. This is First Principles Daily, Episode 160. A six-stop elevator that costs that much here can be put in for the European figure across the Atlantic. The same global manufacturers supply the core components on every continent, yet the finished price splits once the equipment reaches the job site. Steven Smith, 2024 report for the Center for Building in North America, traces that split

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:35.880
to rules, assembly practices, and labor arrangements, not to any difference in steel, motors, or

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:41.180
controllers. Fewer mid-rise buildings get built because each added elevator shaft consumes

00:00:41.180 --> 00:00:46.820
budget that could otherwise support more units or lower rents. Developers facing these numbers

00:00:46.820 --> 00:00:52.060
often default to four-story walk-ups even when land-use rules now permit taller infill.

00:00:52.893 --> 00:00:57.733
North American elevators follow the American Society of Mechanical Engineers Safety Code,

00:00:57.733 --> 00:01:04.833
ASME-A17.1, which diverges from the EN-81 standard used almost everywhere else.

00:01:05.253 --> 00:01:09.973
That separate code requires larger cabin dimensions to accommodate a stretcher and a wheelchair

00:01:09.973 --> 00:01:14.353
turning radius, adding floor area that must be carved out of every shaft.

00:01:15.073 --> 00:01:19.773
The extra space forces thicker structural walls around the hoist way and larger counterweight

00:01:19.773 --> 00:01:23.673
assemblies, both of which increase the steel tonnage per installation.

00:01:23.673 --> 00:01:28.173
manufacturing. Manufacturers therefore produce North American specific variants instead

00:01:28.173 --> 00:01:33.353
of the standardized global models, which shrinks the supplier pool and raises parts prices.

00:01:34.013 --> 00:01:39.573
Installation remains largely on-site and bespoke. European crews routinely assemble two

00:01:39.573 --> 00:01:44.933
floors per week while North American crews average one. The slower pace stems from sequential

00:01:44.933 --> 00:01:50.453
field wiring, custom bracket fabrication, and repeated inspections that cannot be batched,

00:01:50.453 --> 00:01:54.733
Because each jurisdiction interprets the same ASME clauses differently.

00:01:55.173 --> 00:02:00.213
The International Union of Elevator Constructors holds exclusive jurisdiction over most work,

00:02:00.573 --> 00:02:05.293
limiting the labor pool and resisting prefabrication that would move tasks into factories.

00:02:06.013 --> 00:02:08.113
Maintenance contracts follow the same pattern.

00:02:08.713 --> 00:02:15.133
New York developers budget $7,500 a year per unit, while German firms quote around $450.

00:02:15.133 --> 00:02:20.993
dollars. The higher figure covers more frequent on-site testing and proprietary diagnostic tools

00:02:20.993 --> 00:02:26.133
that remain tied to the non-standard North American designs. Because few buildings receive

00:02:26.133 --> 00:02:31.173
elevators at all, the market stays small, which further discourages standardized production.

00:02:31.793 --> 00:02:37.253
High prices limit demand and limited demand keeps prices high. Smith's data show the United

00:02:37.253 --> 00:02:44.173
States has only 3.1 elevators per thousand residents, compared with 27 in Switzerland and 23 in Spain.

00:02:44.173 --> 00:02:51.033
Canada sits at 4.1 per thousand. Developers therefore design around elevators whenever codes allow,

00:02:51.653 --> 00:02:56.913
favoring low-rise walk-ups that need none. When elevators are required, the added cost is

00:02:56.913 --> 00:03:02.717
simply accepted as the price of compliance. The research sources supplied for this episode contain

00:03:02.717 --> 00:03:09.077
no figures for the raw steel, copper, motors, or controllers that make up an elevator, so no magic

00:03:09.077 --> 00:03:13.817
wand floor can be calculated from them. The magic wand number is what the finished thing would

00:03:13.817 --> 00:03:18.457
cost if you paid only for arranging the raw atoms, the price of the materials alone.

00:03:19.137 --> 00:03:24.017
Without those material quantities and commodity prices, any first principles ratio would

00:03:24.017 --> 00:03:26.257
rest on estimates the sources do not support.

00:03:26.877 --> 00:03:30.257
That ratio is the finished price divided by the raw material floor.

00:03:30.937 --> 00:03:34.417
A ratio near one means the thing is already close to its physical limit.

00:03:34.977 --> 00:03:40.057
A large ratio means most of the price sits in design, process, organization, or rules.

00:03:40.057 --> 00:03:45.477
Here, the inputs are missing, so the division cannot be done, and the ratio is not a number

00:03:45.477 --> 00:03:46.457
I am going to invent.

00:03:47.057 --> 00:03:49.737
What the sources do document is where the premium lives.

00:03:50.357 --> 00:03:53.597
It sits entirely in the stages after the hardware leaves the factory.

00:03:54.257 --> 00:03:58.917
Cabin-sized mandates force larger shafts and custom car enclosures that add structural

00:03:58.917 --> 00:04:02.097
steel and finishing work not required under EN81.

00:04:02.977 --> 00:04:07.337
Jurisdiction-specific code interpretations require each installation to receive separate

00:04:07.337 --> 00:04:08.877
engineering review and testing.

00:04:08.877 --> 00:04:11.777
lengthening, permitting, and inspection sequences.

00:04:12.277 --> 00:04:17.397
On-site assembly performed by a narrow pool of union labor stretches schedules and multiplies

00:04:17.397 --> 00:04:18.877
carrying costs for the developer.

00:04:19.697 --> 00:04:25.197
Retrofit work faces the same constraints, which is why China installed 51,000 elevators

00:04:25.197 --> 00:04:30.817
into existing buildings in 2021, while comparable retrofits remain rare in North America.

00:04:31.597 --> 00:04:36.137
Maintenance and modernization follow the same cost curve because replacement parts and service

00:04:36.137 --> 00:04:37.977
protocols remain non-standard.

00:04:37.977 --> 00:04:44.357
The sources therefore locate the entire premium in regulatory fragmentation, labor market restrictions,

00:04:44.717 --> 00:04:50.037
and the absence of factory-finished standardization rather than in the physical content of the machine

00:04:50.037 --> 00:04:50.477
itself.

00:04:51.217 --> 00:04:55.897
One might ask whether the larger cabins deliver measurable safety gains that justify the

00:04:55.897 --> 00:04:56.557
added expense.

00:04:57.317 --> 00:05:01.877
Passenger fatality rates show no corresponding reduction on either side of the Atlantic,

00:05:02.377 --> 00:05:07.017
and worker injury statistics during installation actually run higher under the North American

00:05:07.017 --> 00:05:12.297
regime. The gap is not a verdict on the people who build elevators. It is a measurement

00:05:12.297 --> 00:05:16.557
of where the price sits, and the sources put that place after the factory door.

00:05:17.605 --> 00:05:21.785
Steven Smith Center for Building in North America has already mapped the concrete steps

00:05:21.785 --> 00:05:27.345
that would shrink the gap. Harmonizing North American rules with EN81 would let builders

00:05:27.345 --> 00:05:32.645
adopt the same type 2 cabin dimensions used globally and open the market to the full range

00:05:32.645 --> 00:05:33.985
of international suppliers.

00:05:34.845 --> 00:05:39.345
Prefabricated shaft modules and pre-wired controllers could move the majority of assembly

00:05:39.345 --> 00:05:43.385
into controlled factory conditions, cutting field labor hours by half.

00:05:44.145 --> 00:05:48.645
A larger installed base would support dedicated training pipelines outside the current union

00:05:48.645 --> 00:05:51.525
structure, easing the skilled labor bottleneck.

00:05:52.125 --> 00:05:56.305
Smith notes that even modest moves toward these changes would lower the installed price

00:05:56.305 --> 00:05:59.045
toward the $60,000 range observed in Europe.

00:05:59.045 --> 00:06:07.185
The hardest remaining obstacles are political, rewriting ASME A17.1 to accept EN81 conformity

00:06:07.185 --> 00:06:13.225
assessments, persuading labor organizations to accept factory built units, updating accessibility

00:06:13.225 --> 00:06:16.325
rules that currently embed the larger cabin requirement.

00:06:16.985 --> 00:06:20.825
Countries that have already aligned their codes with the global standards show that safety

00:06:20.825 --> 00:06:23.665
records remain comparable, while costs fall.

00:06:23.665 --> 00:06:28.985
The first visible signal of progress would be a state or province publishing a side-by-side

00:06:28.985 --> 00:06:34.445
comparison of ASME and EN81 requirements with a proposed amendment package.

00:06:34.985 --> 00:06:39.905
Such a document would need to quantify the exact square footage savings per shaft and the

00:06:39.905 --> 00:06:44.185
resulting reduction in structural steel before it could move from analysis to legislative

00:06:44.185 --> 00:06:44.805
text.

00:06:45.005 --> 00:06:50.371
When rules dictate cabin dimensions and assembly locations, the price difference between

00:06:50.371 --> 00:06:54.311
identical machines becomes a map of those rules rather than a map of physics.

00:06:54.931 --> 00:06:59.671
When labor markets and permitting sequences stay jurisdiction specific, the cost curve

00:06:59.671 --> 00:07:03.971
stays steep even though the underlying components are already produced at global scale.

00:07:04.591 --> 00:07:05.891
That is the portable piece.

00:07:06.471 --> 00:07:11.131
A first principle's ratio, when the material floor can be named, tells you whether the

00:07:11.131 --> 00:07:13.931
money is in the atoms or in everything wrapped around them.

00:07:14.471 --> 00:07:19.591
When the sources will not support that floor, the honest move is to say so, and then to locate

00:07:19.591 --> 00:07:24.411
the premium in the stages the record actually describes. Here, those stages are code,

00:07:24.751 --> 00:07:29.491
jurisdiction, and who is allowed to assemble the machine. Tomorrow, the show examines another

00:07:29.491 --> 00:07:34.511
domain where the same separation between material facts and organizational facts creates room

00:07:34.511 --> 00:07:40.351
for a fresh redesign. Which city or province will first publish a draft code that treats EN81

00:07:40.351 --> 00:07:48.011
compliance as equivalent to ASMEA 17.1? That's first principles daily for today. If this changed how

00:07:48.011 --> 00:07:52.111
you see what things cost, share it with someone who depreciated, and subscribe wherever

00:07:52.111 --> 00:07:52.671
you listen.

00:07:53.311 --> 00:07:57.391
I'm Patrick and Vancouver, one example or one opportunity every day.

